const cursor=`BEGIN;
DECLARE order_rows NO SCROLL CURSOR FOR
  SELECT generate_series(101, 105) AS order_id;
FETCH FORWARD 2 FROM order_rows;
-- 101, 102; the consumer stops here
CLOSE order_rows;
ROLLBACK;`;
---
<p>A connection pool keeps database connections open so that requests can borrow them. Borrowing saves the work of establishing a new connection, but it also gives the request something to return. An open transaction, an unfinished result or a setting left by the previous request can outlive the piece of code that created it.</p>
<p>The <a href="/review/relational-next/applications/#borrow-a-connection-for-bounded-work">application article</a> followed Mina’s order through an ordinary borrow, transaction and release. Here the request stops waiting, the result consumer leaves early, or cleanup itself fails. The question at each boundary is the same: who still owns the connection, and what must finish before somebody else may use it?</p>
<p>PostgreSQL 18.6 with node-postgres <code>pg</code> 8.23.1 is the concrete case. Its ordinary, non-pipelined API supplies the observed behaviour below. Other drivers and transaction libraries can make different promises. The figures model ownership; the final section separates them from the real-driver failure checks.</p>

