<h2 id="know-what-a-borrowed-handle-owns">Know what a borrowed handle owns</h2>
<p>A <strong>pool</strong> owns a set of driver clients. A <strong>lease</strong> is one borrower’s temporary ownership of a client. With a direct PostgreSQL connection, that client talks to one database <strong>session</strong>, which holds its transaction, settings and other session state. A transaction belongs to that session; the pool object is not itself a transaction.</p>
<p>For <code>pg</code>, <code>pool.connect()</code> returns a client to use until <code>client.release()</code>. The convenience method <code>pool.query()</code> borrows and returns a client around one query. That is useful for a single independent statement. It does not promise that the next pool call reaches the same session.</p>
<p>Figure 1 starts a transaction on C1. Keep the owning client first, then switch to pool calls and watch where the write goes.</p>
<LifetimeFigure scene="ownership" number={1} />
<p>In the second path, another borrower takes C1 before Mina’s write. The pool sends the write through C2, outside the transaction begun on C1. Rolling back C1 cannot undo it. The native rehearsal reproduced this interleaving with two clients: the inserted row remained after the original transaction rolled back.</p>
<p>Pass the checked-out client into the functions participating in the transaction. If a library gives you a transaction handle, use that handle throughout and establish what it owns. Check whether entering a context starts a transaction, whether leaving normally commits, what an exception does, and whether a handle may be used concurrently. Similar method names in another library are weak evidence.</p>
<p><code>pg</code> does not provide an automatic commit-on-context-exit wrapper. Our checks issue <code>BEGIN</code>, <code>COMMIT</code> and <code>ROLLBACK</code> explicitly. An ordinary standalone statement outside an explicit transaction commits on successful completion. Multi-statement query strings and pipelining have further boundaries; do not use them to guess where one application operation commits.</p>
<p>A single direct session does not run ordinary SQL commands in parallel. The tested non-pipelined client queues commands, but queuing is not a reason to share an owning transaction between unrelated tasks. Their statements can become part of the same transaction, and one task’s rollback affects the others. Keep one accountable owner for the lifetime of the lease.</p>

<h2 id="return-only-a-connection-that-is-ready-for-another-borrower">Return only a connection that is ready for another borrower</h2>
<p>The useful lifecycle is <strong>waiting → acquired → in use → cleaning up → returned or discarded</strong>. Every successful acquisition needs one terminal action. Returning lends the same session again. Discarding closes it so that the pool can create a replacement.</p>
<p>Before returning, finish the old command and its results, end the transaction, and remove any state the next borrower must not inherit. A <code>finally</code> block that calls release establishes where release happens; it does not establish that cleanup happened first.</p>
<p>PostgreSQL distinguishes idle outside a transaction, an open transaction, and a failed transaction. In the tested driver, <code>getTransactionStatus()</code> reports <code>I</code>, <code>T</code> and <code>E</code> after command completion. A division error inside an explicit transaction left <code>E</code>; a subsequent <code>SELECT</code> failed with <code>25P02</code>. After an awaited <code>ROLLBACK</code>, it reported <code>I</code> and another query succeeded.</p>
<p>That status is the last status received from the server, not a live enquiry about an outstanding command. In particular, an earlier <code>I</code> does not establish that a command still in flight is finished. Nor does <code>I</code> say that session settings have been reset.</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Choosing how to finish a lease"><table><thead><tr><th>What the owner knows</th><th>Action before handing over</th></tr></thead><tbody>
 <tr><td>No client was acquired</td><td>Own the pending acquisition through failure or late success; there is no lease to release yet.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Commands completed; an open or failed transaction remains</td><td>Await rollback through the owning API. Verify the session is outside the transaction, then complete state cleanup.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>All work and cleanup completed; reusable state is established</td><td>Return exactly once. In <code>pg</code>, use <code>client.release()</code>.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Connection is broken, cleanup fails, or synchronised state cannot be established within the cleanup allowance</td><td>Dispose through the driver. In <code>pg</code>, <code>client.release(true)</code> removes the client instead of lending it again.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Record an operation failure separately from a cleanup failure. If the write failed first and rollback then lost the connection, retain both facts without replacing the write error with the rollback error. Make the cleanup path terminate in either verified reuse or disposal, including a failure during <code>BEGIN</code>. A rejected syntax error and a lost connection need different cleanup, even if both happened while starting the transaction.</p>
<p>Disposing of a connection protects later borrowers. It does not establish whether a commit reached the database. Keep the operation identity and phase available to <a href="/review/relational-next/request-recovery/">request recovery</a>, which owns reconciliation and decisions about repeating work.</p>

<h3 id="cancellation-is-a-cleanup-sequence">Cancellation is a cleanup sequence</h3>
<p>A caller can stop waiting while the database is still executing. A cancellation request asks the server to stop; it can arrive after the command has finished, fail to arrive, or produce an error that the original connection still has to receive. PostgreSQL’s cancellation contract explicitly separates dispatch from observing the command’s termination.</p>
<p>Figure 2 cancels a statement inside an explicit transaction. Step to the cancellation error, then compare successful rollback, failed cleanup and returning too early.</p>
<LifetimeFigure scene="cleanup" number={2} />
<p>In our native check, a separate administrative connection requested cancellation of a sleeping command in the article’s own session. The command then rejected with <code>57014</code>, the transaction was failed, and the owner awaited rollback before returning the client. This used PostgreSQL’s <code>pg_cancel_backend</code> for controlled fault injection. It is not a proposed request-handler API, and libpq’s C cancellation functions are not methods on the JavaScript client.</p>
<p>The driver’s <code>query_timeout</code> makes a different point. In the tested configuration, the query promise rejected with a read timeout while another connection still observed the server executing <code>pg_sleep</code>. We discarded that client. Treating the early rejection as proof of command completion would have returned a client still doing the old borrower’s work. Check the cancellation and synchronisation contract of the actual API rather than inferring it from a timeout’s name.</p>
<p>Cancellation can also happen before acquisition. Racing <code>pool.connect()</code> against a caller timeout does not remove the underlying pending borrow. If it succeeds later, somebody still has to return the client. Figure 3 has just one slot, so a forgotten late acquisition immediately stops the next request.</p>
<LifetimeFigure scene="acquire" number={3} />
<p>The native rehearsal kept a continuation on the pending borrow and returned its late client without starting business work. It separately verified that this driver’s configured <code>connectionTimeoutMillis</code> removed a queued waiter on expiry. Those are two different mechanisms. Verify whether the chosen acquisition API supports cancellation; do not assume an arbitrary <code>AbortSignal</code> is accepted.</p>
<p>Keep cleanup alive when the original caller goes away. Use the runtime’s supported cancellation shielding, a separately owned cleanup task, or an equivalent mechanism, with a bounded allowance that ends in disposal if cleanup cannot complete. In JavaScript, abandoning a promise does not cancel it: retain its completion and rejection handlers. The operation’s overall deadline and what a timeout means for its business result belong to <a href="/review/relational-next/request-recovery/#spend-one-deadline-across-the-whole-request">request recovery</a>.</p>

<h2 id="match-session-state-to-the-pooling-mode">Match session state to the pooling mode</h2>
<p>A clean transaction boundary does not erase a session. Our one-client pool returned the same session to a new borrower with the previous borrower’s <code>application_name</code> still set. The pool had neither rolled it back nor run a reset command on release. Application code must establish the state it requires.</p>
<p>A tenant setting is a more consequential version of that problem. Prefer transaction-scoped state where the engine and pooling mode support it. Figure 4 compares <code>SET LOCAL</code> inside a transaction with session-level <code>SET</code>. The harmless native check used <code>application_name</code>; the tenant value in the drawing is illustrative.</p>
<LifetimeFigure scene="state" number={4} />
<p><code>SET LOCAL</code> ends with the transaction. That removes one leakage path, but it does not authenticate a tenant, create an access policy or excuse a missing scope predicate. Establish verified context for each operation. The <a href="/review/relational-next/applications/#give-each-application-action-the-authority-it-needs">parent’s authority boundary</a> and <a href="/review/relational-next/security/">service security</a> own those decisions.</p>
<p>A transaction-pooling proxy adds another boundary. The application can hold a client connection to the proxy while successive transactions use different database sessions. State that survived an application lease yesterday may disappear, or belong to another session, after a pooling-mode change.</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="State ownership with direct connections and a transaction-pooling proxy"><table><thead><tr><th>State</th><th>Direct application pool</th><th>PgBouncer transaction pooling</th></tr></thead><tbody>
 <tr><td>Transaction-local setting, ordinary cursor</td><td>Keep the same checked-out client through transaction end.</td><td>Keep the work inside the transaction that owns the backend session.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Session setting, role, temporary object or held cursor needed later</td><td>Can survive lease return; establish a verified reset policy.</td><td>Do not assume the next transaction has that session. Check the feature’s documented compatibility.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Named protocol prepared statement</td><td>The driver tracks it per connection.</td><td>The documented support requires a non-zero <code>max_prepared_statements</code> and a compatible version.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>SQL <code>PREPARE</code> / <code>EXECUTE</code></td><td>SQL objects belong to their session.</td><td>Not covered by that protocol statement tracking.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>This is a compatibility check against PgBouncer’s documented feature map, not a proxy execution result. Record its version, pooling mode and relevant settings. Temporary tables dropped on commit differ from ones preserved across transactions; an ordinary cursor differs from <code>WITH HOLD</code>. Driver caches, protocol preparation and SQL preparation are also distinct. Resetting the server manually can invalidate state a driver thinks it still has, so verify reset and cache behaviour together.</p>

<h2 id="keep-result-consumption-inside-the-resource-lifetime">Keep result consumption inside the resource lifetime</h2>
<p>Mina’s export can be slow even after PostgreSQL has supplied a batch of rows. If the application is waiting for a download destination while keeping a cursor open, the connection remains leased. “SQL finished quickly” does not mean “the pool slot was held briefly”.</p>
<p>The next figure uses a SQL cursor and five invented order identifiers. Fetching two rows leaves three unread. Compare closing before return with returning the lease at the end of the short loop.</p>
<LifetimeFigure scene="stream" number={5} />
<Sql code={cursor} label="The native cursor exercise: leave after two rows, then close before returning" />
<p>During the consumer pause, our separate observer saw <code>idle in transaction</code>. After <code>CLOSE</code>, rollback and release, the next borrower had no cursor named <code>order_rows</code>. No SQL was executing during the pause, but both the lease and transaction still existed.</p>
<p>A pg query promise normally returns a materialised result. Here each <code>FETCH</code> materialises only that batch in the application; the SQL cursor is what retains the session and transaction. A streaming extension may use a protocol portal, buffering and a different close or destroy contract. Check whether early loop exit closes it, how close completion is observed, and who awaits result draining or cancellation before returning the client.</p>
<p>Put full consumption, early exit, consumer error and disconnect through the same ownership boundary. Finish or stop outstanding consumption, await the required close, end its transaction, then return or dispose. A held cursor can survive commit in the same session and materialises its result in temporary storage; it does not become portable to another pooled session.</p>
<p>Bound consumer hold time. Materialising a genuinely bounded result before releasing can be appropriate; materialising an unbounded export trades a lease problem for a memory problem. Shorter retrieval units must preserve the intended ordering and consistency. <a href="/review/relational-next/sql/">Working with SQL</a> owns pagination semantics, and <a href="/review/relational-next/generated-queries/">application query control</a> follows buffering and materialisation through an abstraction. The retained-version costs of a long transaction belong to <a href="/review/relational-next/physical-storage/">stored rows and physical work</a>.</p>

<h2 id="bound-demand-before-enlarging-the-pool">Bound demand before enlarging the pool</h2>
<p>A pool maximum limits clients in that pool. It need not limit queued acquisition requests, incoming requests, background jobs or the sum of every pool in the deployment. Bound admission before the queue can grow without limit, and stop waiting when the caller’s supplied acquisition budget is exhausted.</p>
<p>Borrow after validation and other work that needs no connection. Avoid keeping a lease across an unrelated HTTP call or user think time. The background worker can claim its job in a short transaction and return the client before calling the external service; <a href="/review/relational-next/background-work/">background work and events</a> develops that design.</p>
<p>Figure 6 starts with a supplied allowance of 40 database connections, eight reserved for operation and recovery. Three API processes and four background connections fit. Step into a rolling deployment, then reduce the API’s per-process cap.</p>
<LifetimeFigure scene="demand" number={6} />
<p>At eight each, six overlapping API processes plus four workers can request 52 connections against an application allowance of 32. Four each brings that configured total to 28. These invented counts demonstrate accounting, not a recommended pool size or proof that all maxima are reached together.</p>
<p>Count every process, distinct pool, worker and deployment overlap. Behind a proxy, frontend clients and backend database sessions have separate limits and queues. Use the service allowance and reserve established by <a href="/review/relational-next/monitoring-and-capacity/">monitoring and sizing</a>; do not allocate that reserve again to ordinary work.</p>
<p>If acquisition waits remain after ownership defects are fixed, a larger pool is a hypothesis to measure. It may increase useful concurrency, or increase contention while completing no more work. <a href="/review/relational-next/concurrent-load/">Performance under concurrent load</a> owns that workload experiment.</p>

<h2 id="find-where-the-lease-time-goes">Find where the lease time goes</h2>
<p>Measure acquisition wait separately from lease duration. Within the lease, distinguish database statements from application time holding the client. A request can wait for a slot while all database sessions are idle, because their borrowers are doing something else.</p>
<p>Figure 7 holds the pool occupancy and lease duration constant. Change the owner’s activity and see which investigation follows.</p>
<LifetimeFigure scene="diagnose" number={7} />
<p>Join acquisition start, acquired client, statement spans and terminal release to one borrower identifier. For a direct PostgreSQL connection, record its backend PID and transaction age so that <code>pg_stat_activity</code> can identify the session. A proxy can change the backend assignment; retain its client and server mapping instead of assuming one PID identifies the whole application lease.</p>
<p>Watch queue depth, acquisition timeouts, leased and idle counts, and lease-duration distributions. <code>pg</code> exposes total, idle and waiting counts; it does not thereby provide a complete trace of a borrower. Add operation phase and ownership. A missing release needs a code repair; an old idle transaction needs its owner; slow or blocked SQL belongs with <a href="/review/relational-next/performance/">performance diagnosis</a>. High occupancy alone does not choose among them.</p>
<p>An idle pool connection is also a live network connection. Our rehearsal terminated only its own idle backend: the pool emitted an error, removed that client and created a different session on the next borrow. Attach the documented pool error handler and observe those events. A successful borrow or health check cannot promise that the next statement will succeed; the connection can fail after the check. Validate lifetime and reconnect behaviour without making replacement a reason to repeat an uncertain operation.</p>

<h2 id="prove-cleanup-across-failures-and-shutdown">Prove cleanup across failures and shutdown</h2>
<p>Use a tiny pool in a disposable database. One forgotten lease should stop the test quickly, not hide among hundreds of spare clients. After each injected failure, account for every acquisition, inspect abandoned transactions and settings, then make another borrower execute a known query.</p>
<p>The <a href={rehearsalUrl} download="connection-lifetimes.mjs">real-driver rehearsal</a> ran with Node 24.16.0, <code>pg</code> 8.23.1 and PostgreSQL 18.6, directly connected with pipelining off. It creates and removes its own database. Run it only on a disposable server where the supplied administrative connection may create a database and signal its test sessions; set <code>PG_ADMIN_URL</code>, and install the pinned <code>pg</code> dependency or supply <code>PG_DRIVER_MODULE</code>.</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Observed lifecycle failure checks"><table><thead><tr><th>Injected condition</th><th>Observed evidence</th></tr></thead><tbody>
 <tr><td>Two-client pool transaction split; invalid BEGIN; statement error</td><td>The split write survived the original rollback. Invalid BEGIN left no open transaction. An error inside BEGIN required rollback before reuse.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Borrower leaves session state</td><td>Session SET survived release; SET LOCAL ended at commit and restored the preceding setting.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Caller stops waiting for acquisition</td><td>The pending borrow later succeeded and its retained owner returned it. The configured acquisition timeout separately removed its queued waiter.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Execution cancelled; server statement timeout; driver read timeout</td><td>Cancellation and server timeout produced <code>57014</code> and needed rollback in the explicit transaction. Driver read timeout returned while the server was still active; that connection was discarded.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Cursor consumer stops after two rows</td><td>The observer saw idle in transaction; explicit close and rollback removed the named cursor before the next borrow.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Connection lost before rollback; idle backend disconnected</td><td>Failed rollback led to disposal and a usable replacement. The idle connection was removed and replaced after its pool error.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Shutdown starts with a checked-out client</td><td><code>pool.end()</code> remained pending until the owner disposed of the client; then it completed. Final session count for the test database was zero.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>These checks establish the recorded client and server behaviour, not a deployed HTTP handler’s complete cancellation or shutdown policy. They do not run PgBouncer, a streaming extension, a production network fault or lost-commit reconciliation. The figures’ request timings and connection allowance are illustrative.</p>
<p>For shutdown, stop admission and background acquisition first. Let known owners finish within the service’s grace period, then ask remaining owners to cancel and clean up within a bounded allowance. Dispose of the clients whose safe reuse cannot be established, and await pool closure. Maintain an owner registry so the shutdown path can find those leases.</p>
<p>Do not expect <code>pool.end()</code> alone to enforce the grace period. The native check deliberately held a lease and showed that closure waited. Test the bound on the application’s shutdown coordinator as well as the driver call: an owner that never releases must lead to deliberate disposal or the process supervisor’s bounded termination, rather than an indefinite wait. Closing a socket or process still leaves uncertain business outcomes for the durable operation record and <a href="/review/relational-next/request-recovery/">request recovery</a> to resolve.</p>

